%======================================================================
\chapter{Introduction}

\section{Purpose}
CampusCare is proposed as a centralized web-based system for managing the Health Center at Thapar Institute of Engineering and Technology. The system aims to address the challenges of manual record-keeping, unorganized doctor queues, and difficulties in maintaining and accessing student medical records and prescriptions.

By bringing student registration, check-in, consultations, medical records, prescriptions, and medicine dispensing onto a single platform, CampusCare aims to reduce paperwork, improve the accuracy of information, and make the overall healthcare process more organized and efficient for students and health center staff.

\section{Intended Audience and Reading Suggestions}
This document is intended for developers, project supervisors, testers, and health center staff. It provides an overview of the system, its requirements, and the workflows of different users to support the development, testing, and use of CampusCare.

The chapters follow the usual order of an SRS: introduction, overall description, system features (including external interface requirements), nonfunctional requirements and other requirements. Readers who only want the big picture can read Chapter 1 and Section 2.5. Developers and testers should focus on Chapter 3, which lists the numbered functional requirements.

\section{Project Scope}
CampusCare covers the routine, day-to-day healthcare activities of the health center. Five kinds of people use it: students, doctors, receptionists, pharmacists and administrators. Each gets an interface that shows only what their job needs.

Students can log in to CampusCare using their Thapar email credentials. When they arrive at the health center, they can be identified through an RFID/NFC card or, if the hardware is unavailable, through manual verification by the receptionist. After check-in, a visit is created, and the student is added to the queue of the doctor they have chosen or been assigned to.

Every student also has a personal portal. Its home screen greets the student and summarizes their blood group, number of visits and last visit, shows the dose schedule of the medicines they should take right now, the latest prescription, a health snapshot (conditions and allergies) and an emergency shortcut. From the portal the student can open the full prescription history and the medical history, view the Medical ID, and manage their profile. Where nothing has been recorded yet, the screens say so plainly, for example ``No visits yet'' for a new student.

The receptionist works from a reception dashboard. It shows how many students are waiting in the lounge, in consultation, waiting for medicine and completed today, together with the live status of every doctor's cabin. From it the receptionist handles identification and check-in, watches the live OPD queue, deals with unusual cases such as a student who does not respond when called or one who has to be moved to another doctor, and sees an alert when prescriptions are waiting for pickup at the dispensing station.

The doctor marks themselves available or unavailable, calls the next student, reads the authorized medical history and allergy warnings, records the reason for the visit, the diagnosis and clinical notes, and writes a structured prescription linked to that consultation.

The pharmacist reviews valid prescriptions and records what was actually dispensed. This dispensing record is stored separately, so the doctor's original prescription is never altered at the counter.

The administrator manages user accounts, doctor availability schedules and identification credentials, and can review the audit log.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \figbox{workflow-diagram.png}{13cm}
    \caption{Role and Record Workflow}
    \label{fig:CAMPUSCARE}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:CAMPUSCARE} shows who acts on which record. The administrator manages accounts and sets doctor availability. The receptionist verifies the student, checks the student in so that a visit is created, and handles no-shows and redirection in the doctor's queue. The doctor calls the next student, conducts the consultation and writes the prescription. The pharmacist reviews the prescription and records the dispensing, after which the student collects the medicines.

In short, what one role produces is what the next role works with, which is why the system is designed as one connected workflow instead of separate tools.

%======================================================================
\chapter{Overall Description}

\section{Product Perspective}
CampusCare replaces the manual and partly disconnected way the health center keeps its records today. Information that used to sit in registers, loose papers or separate files is moved into one central database, and every role reads and writes the same records.

The system is a new, self-contained web application. It follows a client-server architecture with three layers:
\begin{center}
React Frontend $\rightarrow$ FastAPI REST API $\rightarrow$ Application Logic $\rightarrow$ MySQL Database
\end{center}
Keeping these layers apart means the interface, the business rules and the storage can each be changed without rewriting the others. The backend is the single authority for the state of the queues, so two staff members working at the same time always see the same picture.

\section{User Classes and Characteristics}
CampusCare has five types of users. Staff is further divided into receptionist and pharmacist.
\begin{itemize}
    \item Students
    \item Doctors
    \item Staff
        \begin{itemize}
            \item Receptionist
            \item Pharmacist
        \end{itemize}
    \item Administrator
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \figbox{user-types.png}{10cm}
    \caption{Type of users}
    \label{fig:type of users}
\end{figure}

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{|p{2.6cm}|p{5cm}|p{5.6cm}|}
\hline
\textbf{User class} & \textbf{Main responsibility} & \textbf{Typical characteristics} \\
\hline
Student & Gets identified, joins a queue, views queue status, dose schedule, prescription history, medical history and Medical ID, and can reach emergency helplines. & Largest group. Uses the system occasionally, mostly on a phone, and needs a very simple interface. \\
\hline
Doctor & Marks availability, calls students, reads history and allergy warnings, records consultations, writes prescriptions. & Uses the system continuously during consultation hours, so speed matters. \\
\hline
Receptionist & Verifies and checks in students, monitors the live queue and doctor cabins, handles no-shows and redirection, and works at the dispensing station. & Works at a front-desk counter during a fixed shift and keeps the reception dashboard open all day. \\
\hline
Pharmacist & Reviews prescriptions and records dispensing. & Works at the dispensing counter and only needs prescription-related screens. \\
\hline
Administrator & Manages users, doctor availability schedules, RFID credentials, audit logs. & Small group with full management rights but no clinical role. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Product Functions}
CampusCare stores and links the visit, queue, consultation, prescription and dispensing records of the students of the campus. Every user must be registered and must log in before using any of these functions.

The system is divided into six processes:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Authentication and User Management
    \item Student Profile and Check-in
    \item Doctor Availability and Queue Management
    \item Consultation and Medical History
    \item Prescription and Dispensing
    \item Administration and Audit
\end{enumerate}

The data behind these processes is organized around the life of one visit:
\begin{center}
Student $\rightarrow$ Visit $\rightarrow$ Queue Entry $\rightarrow$ Consultation $\rightarrow$ Prescription $\rightarrow$ Dispensing
\end{center}

Figure~\ref{fig:DB Schema} (next page) shows how these records are stored. A prescription contains one or more prescription items. A visit belongs to one student and one doctor, and can have at most one consultation.

A visit goes through four stages: \textbf{Waiting}, \textbf{In Consultation}, \textbf{For Medicine} and \textbf{Completed}. The reception dashboard shows the number of students in each stage. Whenever a visit changes its stage, the system records the event, such as when a consultation starts or ends. These events are also shown in the Recent Desk Activity log. The student's home screen also shows the medicine schedule based on the frequency code saved in the prescription, such as 1-0-1 (morning, noon, night).

% The ER diagram is large, so it gets a page of its own to stay readable.
% Export db-schema.png at high resolution (at least 2000 px wide).
