\end{figure}
\clearpage

\section{Operating Environment}
CampusCare runs in a web browser, so it works on Windows, macOS and Linux. The server side runs FastAPI with a MySQL database. RFID/NFC identification needs suitable reader hardware and browser or device support. Where that is missing, the receptionist identifies the student manually, so the health center can keep working either way.

\section{Design}
The workflow of each role is described below.

\textbf{Student.} A student's visit has four steps:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Login and identification (RFID/NFC card or receptionist verification)
    \item Check-in and doctor selection
    \item Waiting in the queue and consultation
    \item Collecting medicines and viewing visit details
\end{itemize}
The student profile holds personal information, medical information, queue status, prescriptions and visit information.

\textbf{Student portal.} After logging in, the student reaches the home screen. The bottom navigation bar has five sections: Home, Prescriptions, Medical History, Emergency and Profile. The home screen includes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item A greeting and a summary of the student's blood group, total visits and last visit date.
    \item \textbf{Dose Schedule}, which shows which medicines the student needs to take and when.
    \item \textbf{Latest Prescription}, with a link to view all previous prescriptions.
    \item \textbf{Health Snapshot}, which shows the student's medical conditions and allergies, along with a link to the Medical ID.
    \item An \textbf{Emergency} card with a button to call 108 and a link to other helpline numbers.
\end{itemize}
The Prescriptions section stores all previous prescriptions, while Medical History shows the student's medical conditions, allergies, current medicines and past visits. Students can access only their own medical records. The activities of a student are summarized in Figure~\ref{fig:Students Activites}.

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \figbox{student_activity_diagram.png}{15cm}
    \caption{Student Activities (Activity Diagram)}
    \label{fig:Students Activites}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Doctor.}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Mark own availability
    \item View the personal queue and call the next student
    \item View authorized medical history and allergy warnings
    \item Record the reason for the visit, diagnosis and clinical notes
    \item Create a structured prescription, print it and send it to the dispensary
    \item Review the consultations completed today
\end{itemize}
The activities of a doctor are summarized in Figure~\ref{fig:Doctor Activities}. The doctor workstation has three tabs in its top bar:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Live OPD Queue.} A side panel shows the doctor's room, the number of students in the queue, the number seen today, shift hours, the average consultation time and a ``Call Next Student'' button. The middle panel lists today's consultation line with each student's roll number, reason for visit, waiting time and status (Inspecting, Next in Line or Waiting). The right panel shows the selected student's medical history, an allergy warning and the clinical chart.
    \item \textbf{E-Prescription Studio.} The doctor adds medicines from one-click formulary presets or as custom entries, chooses the dosage, frequency (for example 1-0-1) and duration, and reviews the table of prescribed medicines. The doctor then enters the final diagnosis and dietary advice, can print the prescription slip, and completes the consultation, which records the prescription under the doctor's name and sends it to the dispensary.
    \item \textbf{Completed Today.} A log of the consultations finished during the shift, showing time, student, roll number, diagnosis, the prescription sent and its dispensary status.
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \figbox{doctor-activity-diagram.png}{15cm}
    \caption{Doctor Activities (Activity Diagram)}
    \label{fig:Doctor Activities}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Receptionist.}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Verify the student and complete check-in
    \item Watch the live OPD queue and open the queue of a particular doctor (``Check Line'')
    \item Handle no-shows and redirect a student to another doctor's queue
    \item Look up students and review completed visits
    \item Open the dispensing station when prescriptions are waiting for pickup
    \item Follow the recent desk activity log
\end{itemize}
The activities of a receptionist are summarized in Figure~\ref{fig:Receptionist Activities}. The reception dashboard has a side menu with Reception, Student Check-in, Live OPD Queue, Students, Dispensing, Completed Visits, Activity, Settings and Logout. The Live OPD Queue and Dispensing entries carry a count badge. The main area contains:
\begin{itemize}
    \item four counters: \textbf{Waiting in Lounge} (with the average wait time), \textbf{In Consultation}, \textbf{For Medicine} and \textbf{Completed Today};
    \item \textbf{Doctor Cabins Live Status}, one card per doctor showing name, specialization, room, whether the doctor is available, and the number of students in that doctor's queue;
    \item an \textbf{Action Required} panel that tells the front desk how many prescriptions are waiting for pickup, with a button to open the dispensing station, and shows the counter and shift timings of the logged-in receptionist;
    \item \textbf{Recent Desk Activity}, a live log with time, student, prescription number or doctor, and the event (for example consultation started or completed).
\end{itemize}

\begin{figure}[H]
    \centering
    \figbox{receptionist-activity-diagram.png}{15cm}
    \caption{Receptionist Activities (Activity Diagram)}
    \label{fig:Receptionist Activities}
\end{figure}

\textbf{Pharmacist.}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Review a valid prescription
    \item Record dispensing against that prescription
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Administrator.}
\begin{itemize}
    \item Create, update and deactivate user accounts
    \item Manage doctor availability schedules and RFID credentials
    \item View system records and audit logs
\end{itemize}

The normal flow of one visit is:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The user logs in and the student's identity is verified.
    \item The receptionist checks the student in and a visit is created.
    \item The student is added to the chosen doctor's queue.
    \item The doctor calls the next student and opens the authorized medical history.
    \item The doctor records the consultation and, if needed, creates a prescription.
    \item The visit moves to the For Medicine stage and shows up as a pickup alert on the reception dashboard. The pharmacy side reviews the prescription and records the dispensing.
    \item The visit is marked completed and stays available for future authorized access.
\end{enumerate}

%======================================================================
\chapter{System Features}
CampusCare is a healthcare workflow web application. Its main job is to carry a student's visit from check-in to dispensing without the information being copied by hand from one step to the next.

\section{Description and Priority}
Some features are central and some are supporting, but every one of them is needed for the system to be complete. In order of priority, from highest to lowest:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Authentication and role-based access:} every user logs in and sees only what their role allows. Nothing else works safely without this.
    \item \textbf{Check-in and queue management:} this is the heart of the system. Each doctor has a separate queue with position and status, and the receptionist can handle no-shows and redirect students.
    \item \textbf{Consultation and medical history:} the doctor reads the authorized history and allergy warnings, and records the reason, diagnosis and notes.
    \item \textbf{Prescription:} the doctor creates a structured prescription tied to the consultation and sends it to the dispensary.
    \item \textbf{Dispensing:} the pharmacist records dispensing against a valid prescription, separately from the prescription itself.
    \item \textbf{Role dashboards:} the student portal (prescription history, medical history, dose schedule, Medical ID, emergency shortcut), the doctor workstation (live queue, e-prescription studio, completed log) and the reception dashboard (live counters, doctor cabin status, pickup alerts, desk activity).
    \item \textbf{Administration and audit:} the administrator manages users, doctor availability and credentials, and important actions are logged.
    \item \textbf{RFID/NFC identification:} an extension that speeds up check-in where the hardware is available.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Functional Requirements}
The functional requirements are listed below. ``Shall'' marks a requirement that must be met, and ``may'' marks an optional one. The Priority column uses three levels: \textbf{Must} (essential for the system to work), \textbf{Should} (important, but the system can run without it for a short time) and \textbf{May} (optional).

\begin{longtable}{|p{1.3cm}|>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{3cm}|>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{6.3cm}|p{1.4cm}|}
\hline
\textbf{ID} & \textbf{Requirement} & \textbf{Description} & \textbf{Priority} \\
\hline
\endfirsthead
\hline
\textbf{ID} & \textbf{Requirement} & \textbf{Description} & \textbf{Priority} \\
\hline
\endhead
FR-01 & Authentication & The system shall authenticate users and give access according to their assigned role. & Must \\
\hline
FR-02 & Student Profile & The system shall maintain student profile and identification information. & Must \\
\hline
FR-03 & Student Check-in & The receptionist shall be able to verify and check in a student. & Must \\
\hline
FR-04 & Doctor Availability & The system shall maintain doctor availability information. The doctor shall be able to mark themselves available or unavailable, and the administrator shall be able to manage availability schedules. & Must \\
\hline
FR-05 & Queue Management & The system shall maintain a separate queue for each doctor. & Must \\
\hline
FR-06 & Queue Status & The system shall maintain queue position and status information (for example Inspecting, Next in Line, Waiting). & Must \\
\hline
FR-07 & Medical History & An authorized doctor shall be able to access the relevant medical history of a student. & Must \\
\hline
FR-08 & Consultation & The doctor shall be able to record the reason for consultation, the diagnosis and clinical notes. & Must \\
\hline
FR-09 & Prescription & The doctor shall be able to create a structured prescription linked to the consultation. & Must \\
\hline
FR-10 & Dispensing & The pharmacist, or reception staff working at the dispensing station, shall be able to record medicine dispensing against a valid prescription. & Must \\
\hline
FR-11 & Administration & The administrator shall be able to manage users, doctor availability and RFID credentials. & Must \\
\hline
FR-12 & RFID/NFC & The system may support RFID/NFC-based identification where suitable hardware and browser support are available. & May \\
\hline
FR-13 & Audit & Important administrative and system actions shall be recorded for auditing. & Should \\
\hline
FR-14 & No-show Handling & The queue system shall support handling of students who do not respond when called. & Should \\
\hline
FR-15 & Queue Redirection & The receptionist or the doctor shall be able to redirect a student to an appropriate doctor's queue when required. & Should \\
\hline
FR-16 & Student Home & The student portal shall show the student's blood group, number of visits, last visit, dose schedule, latest prescription and health snapshot, with clear empty states when nothing is recorded. & Should \\
\hline
FR-17 & Prescription History & A student shall be able to view all of their own earlier prescriptions. & Should \\
\hline
FR-18 & Student Medical History & A student shall be able to view their own medical history and Medical ID (conditions, allergies, ongoing medication, past visits). & Should \\
\hline
FR-19 & Dose Schedule & The system shall list the medicines due at the current time of day, based on the frequency code recorded in the prescription. & Should \\
\hline
FR-20 & Emergency Access & The student portal shall provide a one-tap call to the emergency number (108) and a list of helplines. & Should \\
\hline
FR-21 & Reception Dashboard & The system shall show the receptionist the number of students waiting, in consultation, waiting for medicine and completed today, with the average waiting time. & Should \\
\hline
FR-22 & Doctor Cabin Status & The system shall show each doctor's room, availability status and queue length, and let the receptionist open that doctor's queue. & Should \\
\hline
FR-23 & Pickup Alert & The system shall alert the front desk when prescriptions are waiting for pickup and give direct access to the dispensing station. & Should \\
\hline
FR-24 & Desk Activity Log & The system shall keep a log of recent visit events (for example consultation started and completed) and show it on the reception dashboard. & Should \\
\hline
FR-25 & Student Directory & The receptionist shall be able to look up students and review completed visits. & Should \\
\hline
FR-26 & Allergy Warning & The system shall show a prominent warning of the student's known allergies to the doctor before a prescription is written. & Must \\
\hline
FR-27 & Formulary Presets & The prescription screen shall offer one-click presets of commonly used medicines, and shall also allow a custom medicine to be added. & Should \\
\hline
FR-28 & Prescription Item Details & Each prescription item shall record the medicine name, dosage, a frequency code (for example 1-0-1 or OD) and the duration. General instructions such as food notes and dietary advice shall be recorded on the prescription. & Must \\
\hline
FR-29 & Prescription Slip & The doctor shall be able to print a prescription slip. & Should \\
\hline
FR-30 & Dispatch to Dispensary & When the doctor completes the consultation, the system shall record the prescription under that doctor's name and send it to the dispensary in real time. & Must \\
\hline
FR-31 & Doctor Completed Log & The doctor shall be able to view the consultations completed today, with time, student, roll number, diagnosis, medicines sent and dispensary status. & Should \\
\hline
FR-32 & Doctor Metrics & The doctor workstation shall show the number of students in the queue, the number seen today, the shift hours (from the availability schedule) and the average consultation time. & Should \\
\hline
\end{longtable}

\textbf{Technology.} CampusCare is built with the following tools.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Front-end:} React with Vite, Tailwind CSS and JavaScript.
    \item \textbf{Back-end:} FastAPI (Python) exposing a REST API.
    \item \textbf{Database:} MySQL, with schema changes managed through version-controlled migrations.
    \item \textbf{Identification:} staff-assisted check-in, with RFID/NFC support planned.
    \item \textbf{Testing:} unit and integration testing.
\end{itemize}

\section{External Interface Requirements}
\textbf{User interfaces.} CampusCare is used through a web browser. The student portal is designed mainly for phones, with a bottom navigation bar. The doctor workstation and the reception dashboard are designed for desktop screens: the doctor workstation uses a top tab bar, and the reception dashboard a side menu. Every screen shows a plain message when there is nothing to display.

\textbf{Hardware interfaces.}
\begin{itemize}
    \item An optional RFID/NFC card reader at the reception desk for student identification.
    \item A printer at the doctor's workstation for the prescription slip.
    \item On a phone, the emergency button starts a normal phone call to the number 108.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Software interfaces.} The React front end talks to the FastAPI back end through a REST API that exchanges JSON. The back end reads and writes the MySQL database. No other external software system is required.

\textbf{Communication interfaces.} All traffic between the browser and the server should use HTTPS. The queue, the dashboard counters and the desk activity log are refreshed automatically so that every user sees the same state.

%======================================================================
\chapter{Other Nonfunctional Requirements}

\section{Performance Requirements}
Everyday operations such as login, check-in, queue updates and record lookup should respond fast enough that nobody at the front desk or in the consultation room has to wait on the system. The targets below are for normal health-center load. They are starting targets and will be confirmed or adjusted after testing under realistic conditions.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{NFR-01:} Login, student lookup and opening a medical record: within 3 seconds for at least 95\% of requests.
    \item \textbf{NFR-02:} Check-in and calling the next student: within 2 seconds.
    \item \textbf{NFR-03:} A change in the queue, dashboard counters, cabin status or activity log should appear on other screens within 5 seconds.
    \item \textbf{NFR-04:} The system should support at least 50 users logged in at the same time.
    \item \textbf{NFR-05:} The system should be available during the health center's working hours, with a target of at least 99\%.
\end{itemize}

\section{Security Requirements}
CampusCare stores medical information, so access has to be controlled carefully.
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{NFR-06:} Only registered users can enter the system, and every user must log in.
    \item \textbf{NFR-07:} Access is role-based. A user can perform only the actions that belong to their role, for example a pharmacist cannot open a consultation note and a student cannot see another student's record.
    \item \textbf{NFR-08:} Passwords are stored only as hashes, never as plain text.
    \item \textbf{NFR-09:} A doctor is authorized to open the medical history only of students who have a visit assigned to that doctor.
    \item \textbf{NFR-10:} Important administrative actions are written to the audit log.
    \item \textbf{NFR-11:} Data between the browser and the server is sent over HTTPS.
\end{itemize}

