\section{Software Quality Attributes}
Testing and feedback from users continue during development, so quality is checked along the way and not left to the end. Database tests, logic tests and user-interface tests are all required, and the system is tested through functional, integration and role-based testing.

The qualities we aim for are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Reliability:} student, consultation, prescription and dispensing data stay consistent and available during normal operation.
    \item \textbf{Usability:} the interfaces are simple enough for students and staff to use without training.
    \item \textbf{Maintainability:} the application is modular, so one component can be changed without disturbing unrelated ones.
    \item \textbf{Auditability:} important administrative and system activities can be traced afterwards.
    \item \textbf{Data integrity:} a prescription is never overwritten by dispensing, because the two are stored as separate records.
\end{itemize}

\section{Business Rules}
\begin{itemize}
    \item A student must be identified and checked in before a visit is created.
    \item Each doctor has a separate queue, and a student can be moved to another doctor's queue only by the receptionist or the doctor.
    \item A prescription must be linked to a consultation, and a dispensing record must be linked to a valid prescription.
    \item The prescription stays as the doctor wrote it. Dispensing is recorded separately, by the pharmacist or by reception staff working at the dispensing station.
    \item A student's known allergies are shown to the doctor before a prescription is written.
    \item The backend is the single source of truth for the queue state.
    \item A visit passes through the stages Waiting, In Consultation, For Medicine and Completed, and every stage change is recorded as a visit event.
    \item A visit appears in the pickup alert only after the consultation is completed and a prescription exists.
    \item A student can view only their own prescriptions and medical records.
    \item Where nothing is recorded (no visits, no prescriptions, no conditions), the screens show a plain ``none recorded'' message instead of leaving blanks.
    \item Visit information stays available for future authorized access.
\end{itemize}
The overall aim is to save the health center's working time and reduce pressure on its staff.

%======================================================================
\chapter{Other Requirements}
CampusCare is meant to stay in use for a long time, so it will need regular maintenance and refactoring, and its requirements may change as the health center's needs change. Database changes are made through version-controlled migration files so that they can be tracked and repeated on every development machine.

Because the health center depends on the system during its working hours, the availability of the backend and the database has to be planned for at deployment.

Possible future work includes broader RFID/NFC integration, recording of vitals (blood pressure, pulse, SpO2 and temperature), better real-time queue updates, medication reminders, de-identified health trends, advanced reporting and links to other campus healthcare services.

